  "summary": "New genome-linked resources span food plasmids, digesters, soil and gut isolates, alongside a long-read strain-profiling benchmark for such catalogs.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is genome-resolved resource building that ties microbial sequences to ecological context and inferred function. PLATE assembles plasmid contig catalogs from 4,690 food metagenomes with host, taxonomy and AMR/functional annotations, and CAnDi catalogues anaerobic digestion microbiomes. Functional inference from genomes appears in 550 soil MAG-derived metabolic models, where FBA predicts conserved amino acid and fermentation fluxes, and in gut isolate comparisons showing expanded proteomes and fluid pangenomes in industrialized-host strains. Sourmash is benchmarked on long-read mock metagenomes, with gene content recovery F1 of 0.942-0.998, supporting strain-level profiling against such references. The items differ in scale and method, so the shared emphasis is on curated, annotated genome collections rather than a single technique.",
